other, Domingo de Guzmán was the founder of the Order of Preachers, whom he helped in his reforming aims throughout his life.", "newValue": "Manés de Guzmán, born 1166 in Caleruega, Castile, and died 1237, was a Castilian Dominican priest beatified in the Roman Catholic Church. His primary significance stems from his foundational role in assisting his younger brother, Domingo de Guzmán (Saint Dominic), in establishing the Order of Preachers (Dominicans) during the early 13th century, a period of intense religious reform and emergent mendicant orders. He was a key figure in the early spiritual and administrative development of the nascent order, particularly in its evangelistic and anti-heretical missions in southern France.

Manés de Guzmán actively supported Saint Dominic from the Order's inception. He was among the initial small group of nine companions who, in 1215, presented their rule of life to Bishop Fulk of Toulouse and were granted permission to preach. Manés was instrumental in the early mission to combat Catharism in Languedoc, participating in debates and preaching tours alongside his brother. Notably, he was sent to Paris by Saint Dominic in 1217 as one of the first sixteen Dominicans to establish a presence at the University of Paris, a crucial move for the Order's intellectual and academic development and its expansion across Europe.

